WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.588
Here are the two
How-sections side by side.

2
00:00:02.588 --> 00:00:07.791
The audit phase: the compliant network
policy runs in report-only for ten business

3
00:00:07.791 --> 00:00:12.765
days, reading the would-be blocks; tenant
restrictions have no report-only mode,

4
00:00:12.765 --> 00:00:17.050
so the pilot group is the audit phase,
with the block query read daily.

5
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:22.029
Enforcement: the compliant network policy's
state goes to enabled for the pilot,

6
00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:23.689
then ring groups are added;

7
00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:27.612
tenant restrictions widen with
the Microsoft profile's rings.

8
00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:32.659
Rollback: the policy goes back to
report-only, and in an outage the break-glass

9
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:38.025
script does it; for tenant restrictions,
tagging is disabled and the policy stays.

10
00:00:38.025 --> 00:00:42.986
Exclusions: the emergency access group,
tested each quarter, and the Intune apps,

11
00:00:42.986 --> 00:00:47.421
with service accounts handled by
workload identity policies instead;

12
00:00:47.421 --> 00:00:50.653
tenant restrictions have
none at the tagging layer,

13
00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:54.862
and partner allowances are policies
with an owner and a review date.

14
00:00:54.862 --> 00:00:59.859
Telemetry: the sign-in query and the token
or device inconsistency alert, and for

15
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.661
tenant restrictions
the block query and the

16
00:01:02.661 --> 00:01:05.387
increased external tenant activity alert.

17
00:01:05.387 --> 00:01:10.024
On day two, the security monitor watches
compliant network blocks that didn't come

18
00:01:10.024 --> 00:01:13.706
through the service, which are
either an attack or a broken client,

19
00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:17.450
and the identity engineer reviews
the partner list each quarter.
